direction: right

title: |md
  ## Kernel stack vs user stack walking
| {near: top-center; shape: text}

kstack: "Kernel stack\n`bpf_get_stackid(ctx, m, 0)`" {
  style.fill: "#dbeafe"
  ks_what: |md
    Walks kernel ip frames using:
    - **ORC unwinder** (default on x86_64 since 4.14)
    - or frame pointers (`CONFIG_FRAME_POINTER`)

    ✅ Always available in kernel context
    ✅ Symbolized via /proc/kallsyms
    ✅ Cheap (~1µs)

    Frame format: array of kernel virtual addresses
  |
}

ustack: "User stack\n`bpf_get_stackid(ctx, m, BPF_F_USER_STACK)`" {
  style.fill: "#fef3c7"
  us_what: |md
    Walks **userspace** stack of current task:
    - Requires `CONFIG_FRAME_POINTER` in user binary,
      OR DWARF unwind info (slower, kernel can't use)

    ⚠️ Userspace stacks often lack frame pointers
        (gcc -fomit-frame-pointer is default on amd64)
    ⚠️ JIT'd languages (JS, Java) need extra symbol info

    ✅ Symbolize via /proc/PID/maps + ELF symbols
    ✅ Reveals "which function in your app called read()"

    Frame format: array of user virtual addresses
  |
}

both: "Both at once\n`bpf_get_stack(ctx, buf, sz, 0)`\n+ `bpf_get_stack(ctx, buf, sz, USER)`" {
  style.fill: "#dcfce7"
  both_what: |md
    Use **two separate calls** to capture
    a **mixed** kernel+user stack
    (kernel frames stack on top of the
    syscall entry, user frames below).

    Combine in userspace before symbolizing.
  |
}

flag: "Flags:\n- 0 (kernel)\n- BPF_F_USER_STACK\n- BPF_F_FAST_STACK_CMP\n- BPF_F_REUSE_STACKID" {
  style.fill: "#f3f4f6"
}

kstack -> flag {style.stroke-dash: 4}
ustack -> flag {style.stroke-dash: 4}
both -> flag {style.stroke-dash: 4}
